*   **"Carbon language"**: Carbon was announced by Google in **September 2022**. By late 2023, it was a known "C++ compatible" alternative being discussed, but not yet widely adopted. The author mentions it as a future possibility, which fits the post-announcement, pre-adoption phase.
*   **"Type alias impl trait" (TAIT)**: The author mentions TAIT as "forthcoming." In Rust, TAIT was stabilized in **Rust 1.79**, released in **June 2024**. However, it was in nightly and heavily discussed in 2023. The phrasing "forthcoming" suggests it was not yet stable at the time of writing. If this were written in mid-2024, the author might have said "recently stabilized" or "now available." The "sedate" pace comment also reflects the post-2022 Rust community sentiment where major language features had slowed down compared to the early 2010s.

